# Currentness and write scope

## One lineage

Before acting from any file, resolve the current pointer chain starting at
`operations/README.md`. Never choose a file by its name, its date or its
apparent recency. A superseded file is evidence, not a launch surface.

If two files each claim to be current for the same scope, that is a
currentness break. Stop and repair the break. Do not pick one, do not write a
third file that reconciles them in prose, and do not proceed on the assumption
that the newer one wins.

Run `python3 scripts/check_operator_taxonomy.py` before every commit. This is
enforced by `.cursor/hooks/guard_write_scope.py`, so a failing check refuses
the commit rather than producing a CI failure after the commit already exists.

## One writer per surface

A shared projection surface has exactly one declared writer. Everything else
works on an isolated branch and returns it.

`.cursor/write-scope.json` is the single place protected branches and paths
are named. The guard reads that file. Do not restate a boundary in prose
somewhere else — two statements of one boundary is how they drift apart.

Never push to, commit on, or rewrite the history of a protected branch. If you
believe a boundary is wrong for your task, say so and stop. Changing
`.cursor/write-scope.json` is an explicit decision, never a step inside a task.

## Scope of a commission

A commission owns a bounded problem, not the whole operation. Before starting,
state which existing commission your work sits inside or which one it
supersedes, and record the supersession. Two commissions that both claim the
whole operation is the same failure as two files that both claim to be current.

## Changing the mechanism

A lesson recorded in a document changes nothing. When you find a recurring
failure, the deliverable is the mechanism that makes it not recur — a hook, a
validator, a schema, a test — plus the evidence it fires. If you can only
describe the lesson, say that you could not mechanise it and why.
